# Gradle (top-level only — unscoped `build/` would clobber the Java
# package at org.jawata.mcp/.../tools/build/ shipped in Sprint 13).
.gradle/
/build/
# Note: gradle/, gradlew, gradlew.bat are NOT ignored - they should be committed
# so others can build without installing Gradle

# IDE
.idea/
*.iml
.vscode/
.settings/
.project
.classpath
*.swp
*~

# ...but a TEST FIXTURE's .project/.classpath are test DATA, not a developer's
# Eclipse artifacts. Sprint 28 (v3.6.1): the pde-nonstandard-layout fixture
# exists to reproduce an Eclipse plug-in layout, and its .classpath is what
# declares `test/` as a source folder. The rules above silently dropped it from
# the commit, so the fixture built fine locally and arrived in CI without the
# one file that makes it a PDE project — the two regression tests then failed
# on the release run for exactly the reason they were written to catch.
!org.jawata.core.tests/test-resources/sample-projects/**/.classpath
!org.jawata.core.tests/test-resources/sample-projects/**/.project

# FOURTH occurrence, same class (Sprint 28, C1 re-audit): `.settings/` above
# swallowed plain-eclipse's org.eclipse.jdt.core.prefs — the ONLY file in which
# that fixture declares compliance=17, since it has no pom, no gradle and no
# manifest. Its "the declared level is applied" test would therefore have
# passed here and failed in every clone. A directory rule must be negated as a
# directory before anything inside it can be re-included.
!org.jawata.core.tests/test-resources/sample-projects/**/.settings/
!org.jawata.core.tests/test-resources/sample-projects/**/.settings/**

# Build outputs
*.jar
*.class
out/
target/
# Sprint 11 Phase C: embedded Bundle-ClassPath jars must be tracked
!org.jawata.core/lib/*.jar

# Sprint 28 (C1 audit), THIRD occurrence of the *.jar rule silently eating a
# tracked artifact (after 8b9f8eb and the patched-bundles line below): the
# jars a D-IMPORTER fixture needs to prove "dependency jars reach the
# classpath" were never committed, so that assertion passed on the author's
# machine and would have found nothing in a fresh clone. Each is a few hundred
# bytes and built from a source file beside it.
!org.jawata.core.tests/test-resources/sample-projects/**/*.jar

# …but NOT inside a fixture's own OUTPUT tree (C1, audit round 5). The negation
# above re-exposed exactly what the Maven narrowing below was written to shut:
# a Gradle or Bazel run in a fixture directory drops jars into build/libs or
# bazel-out, and the next `git add -A` would commit them. The Bazel fixture's
# bazel-bin/ jar is a deliberate, committed exception and is re-negated after.
org.jawata.core.tests/test-resources/sample-projects/*/build/**
org.jawata.core.tests/test-resources/sample-projects/*/bazel-out/**
org.jawata.core.tests/test-resources/sample-projects/*/.gradle/**
!org.jawata.core.tests/test-resources/sample-projects/*/build/generated/**
!org.jawata.core.tests/test-resources/sample-projects/simple-bazel/bazel-bin/**

# The output-exclusion TRAPS. A fixture proves "build output is not mounted as
# source" only if there IS output to mount, so each fixture plants a .java under
# the directory its build system writes to. `target/` above would eat the Maven
# one — and a trap that is not in the repository turns its test into a
# tautology that passes on the broken code. Scoped to the one fixture that
# needs it, which nothing builds, so no real output lands here.
# NARROWED to the trap subtree (C1 audit round 3). Un-ignoring the whole
# target/ exposed it to `mvn dependency:build-classpath`, which the loader runs
# IN the fixture directory and which writes a jawata-classpath-<pid>-<nonce>.txt
# there — normally deleted, but two leftovers from July prove that is not
# guaranteed. Under the wide negation the next `git add -A` would have committed
# run residue: the gate's own "nothing enters our tree" clause, broken by the
# change that closed it.
!org.jawata.core.tests/test-resources/sample-projects/maven-level-17/target/
org.jawata.core.tests/test-resources/sample-projects/maven-level-17/target/*
!org.jawata.core.tests/test-resources/sample-projects/maven-level-17/target/classes-src/
!org.jawata.core.tests/test-resources/sample-projects/maven-level-17/target/classes-src/**

# Working folder - session continuity docs (not tracked)
working/

# OS files
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Logs
*.log
logs/

# Temp files
*.tmp
*.bak
*.javalens.bak

# Environment
.env
.env.local

# JavaLens Eclipse workspace metadata (generated when using javalens MCP)
.javalens-workspace/

# MCP configuration (machine-specific)
.mcp.json

# npm wrapper
npm/node_modules/
npm/dist/

# Claude Code configuration (machine-specific)
.claude/
org.jawata.launcher/dependency-reduced-pom.xml
docs/REFACTORING_LESSONS_LEARNED.md

# 22c: the #5188-patched compiler.batch bundle must be tracked (Directory target
# location) — the *.jar rule silently dropped it from 8b9f8eb; sentinel caught it on CI
!org.jawata.target/patched-bundles/*.jar
!org.jawata.target/carry-bundles/*.jar

# JVM attach handshake litter (a target held at start never consumes it)
.attach_pid*

# jawata per-workspace data (quality-sweep baselines, coverage + runtime
# artifacts) — internal analysis state, never published
.jawata/
__pycache__/

# SIXTH occurrence (Sprint 28, C1 re-audit): `/build/` above ate
# build/probe-public-bazel.sh — the D-IMPORTER probe the Stage-1 commit claimed
# to deliver existed only in the working tree. The reactor and its scripts live
# under build/; only its OUTPUT should be ignored, and target/ + *.jar + *.class
# below already do that. Un-ignore the directory so a new script or module pom
# is VISIBLE in git status instead of silently dropped.
!/build/
